    {
      "id": "el70",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 60,
      "y": 655,
      "width": 1344,
      "height": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1070,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 2070,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. All three grounds must connect: controller, MAX485 and S3. A missing ground gives noise or silence.\n2. Pin numbers are counted the way this cable was wired. SRNE's manual counts from the other end, so 7, 6, 5 here are 2, 3, 4 in the manual.\n3. Use S3 3V3 for VCC, not 5 V: the S3's pins aren't rated for 5 V signals from RO.\n4. Don't connect anything to the S3's RX or TX pins, because the COM port uses them.\n5. Power everything off before moving these wires. The controller's RS485 port has hung until it was power-cycled.",
      "originalText": "1. All three grounds must connect: controller, MAX485 and S3. A missing ground gives noise or silence.\n2. Pin numbers are counted the way this cable was wired. SRNE's manual counts from the other end, so 7, 6, 5 here are 2, 3, 4 in the manual.\n3. Use S3 3V3 for VCC, not 5 V: the S3's pins aren't rated for 5 V signals from RO.\n4. Don't connect anything to the S3's RX or TX pins, because the COM port uses them.\n5. Power everything off before moving these wires. The controller's RS485 port has hung until it was power-cycled.",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true
    }
